@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-customisation Customisation
@brief Reporting the application types, and writing application comparators.

@details
The applications extend the framework in two ways, both with public names
only: they teach the formatter to write the values of their own types, and
they write their own comparators and predicates, which are reported as the
built-in ones.

@par Application types
The function comparators report the values of their operands when a check
fails. The scalars, the strings, the containers, and the scoped
enumerations are written by the framework. For any other type, the
application provides an `operator<<` that writes the value to the
`expression_formatter`, declared in the namespace of the type, so that it
is found by argument-dependent lookup. Without it, a comparator such as
`eq()` does not compile for that type. The same operator writes the values
appended to the messages of the checks.

@par Application comparators and predicates
A comparator is an expression template, parametrised by a tag of the
application: a type with a `symbol` text, which separates the operands in
the report, and a static `apply()` function, which returns the result.
`extension::compare_op` takes two operands and a tag, and
`extension::predicate_op` takes one operand and a tag. The result is an
expression like those returned by `eq()` and `negate()`, so it can be
combined with `all_of()`, `any_of()`, and `negate()`, and a failed check
displays the operands and the symbol.

The concepts of the `extension` namespace (`is_op`, `any_op`, `checkable`,
`printable`, `compare_tag`, and `predicate_tag`) describe the types that
these templates and the checks accept.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

namespace app
{
  struct point
  {
    int x;
    int y;
  };

  constexpr bool
  operator== (const point& lhs, const point& rhs)
  {
    return lhs.x == rhs.x && lhs.y == rhs.y;
  }

  // Found by argument-dependent lookup.
  inline mt::expression_formatter&
  operator<< (mt::expression_formatter& formatter, const point& p)
  {
    return formatter << "{ " << p.x << ", " << p.y << " }";
  }

  struct divides_tag
  {
    static constexpr const char* symbol = " divides ";

    template <class Lhs_T, class Rhs_T>
    static constexpr bool
    apply (const Lhs_T& lhs, const Rhs_T& rhs)
    {
      return rhs % lhs == 0;
    }
  };

  template <class Lhs_T, class Rhs_T>
  constexpr auto
  divides (const Lhs_T& lhs, const Rhs_T& rhs)
  {
    return mt::extension::compare_op{ lhs, rhs, divides_tag{} };
  }
} // namespace app

ts.test ("Check points", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (app::point{ 1, 2 }, app::point{ 1, 3 })) << "differ";
});

ts.test ("Check divisors", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (app::divides (5, 12)) << "5 divides 12";
});
@endcode

The failed checks are reported with the values and the symbol:

@code{.console}
    • Check points
        ✗ differ FAILED (main.cpp:75, { 1, 2 } == { 1, 3 })
    • Check divisors
        ✗ 5 divides 12 FAILED (main.cpp:80, 5 divides 12)
@endcode

A predicate is written in the same way, with a tag that has a `symbol`
and an `apply()` function of one operand, and the template
`extension::predicate_op`.
